// items.cpp - E22a / decision 38 (approved). See items.h for what this is for.
//
// THREADING - the same shape as worldstate.cpp, and for the same reason. The four engine functions hooked
// below are reached from the UI, from AI tasks, from building ticks and from the load path, and NOTHING here
// establishes which thread any of them runs on (read-inventory.md "Not established" 5 says so in terms). So a
// detour does the MINIMUM: guarded POD reads, no allocation, no lock, no logging, no engine call - and a push
// into a fixed 512-slot ring under an InterlockedIncrement ticket. Every detour also books which thread it ran
// on, so the claim above becomes a measurement rather than an assumption.
//
// THE OWNERSHIP TEST IS NOT IN THE DETOUR. `net::IsUidMine` reads `g_localOwned`, a std::set that the session
// layer mutates on the main thread, so asking it from a worker would be a tree walk racing an insert (the E20
// finding, read-e20.md 2). `FindSpawnedUid` is address-compare only and is safe anywhere, but it is pointless
// without IsUidMine. So the detour rings the CHARACTER POINTER and the main-thread drain (ItemsTick) asks both
// questions and sends.
//
// WHICH FOUR, AND WHY THOSE (read-inventory.md 2.2, F530 - all four RVAs re-resolved from
// RE_Kenshi/RVAs/Steam_1.0.65.br with tools/resolve_stub.py - read-inventory.md 0):
//   A  InventorySection::_addItem                    0x748BE0  an item enters a grid slot
//   B  InventorySection::removeItem                  0x748EE0  an item leaves a section
//   C  InventoryItemBase::addQuantity                0x75D6E0  a stack absorbs another stack - NO add/remove fires
//   D  Inventory::takeItemOut  0x749E10  a stack is SPLIT - a clone appears, again no add/remove
// Every UI drag, right-click auto-trade, AI loot, trade, restock, death-drop and load path passes through one
// of these. D also handles the whole-stack removal, but that path calls B itself, so D only rings the SPLIT.
//
// WHOSE INVENTORY IS IT. Exactly four classes own an Inventory (read-inventory.md 1.4). The test used here is
// a POD back-pointer identity check, because it needs no engine call and cannot be fooled by a vtable that
// moved: the Inventory's `owner` (+0x88) is read, and the owner is a CHARACTER when the pointer stored at
// owner+0x2E8 - Character::getInventory 0x5E1450 returns exactly `*(char+0x2E8)` - IS THIS INVENTORY. The two
// container classes are tested the same way first (ContainerItem +0x290, UseableStuff +0x430, in ascending
// offset order so a small object is never read past its end) and are booked as `ownerNotChar`. Anything else -
// a ShopTraderInventory, an owner we cannot read - is `ownerUnknown` and NOTHING is done. Both are silent by
// design: E22a is character inventories only.
//
// RE-ENTRANCY. The apply calls the very functions this file hooks, on the main thread. `g_itApplyingThread`
// carries the APPLYING THREAD's id rather than a bool, exactly as worldstate.cpp's g_wsApplyingThread does, so
// a genuine move on a worker thread is not swallowed as if it were our own echo.
