// E22b-2 (P6b) - THE TWO GUI ENDS OF A MOUSE DRAG, and the game's own record of what is on the cursor.
// review-p5y HIGH-1: the four hooks above see a drag as a REMOVE at mouse-DOWN and an ADD at mouse-UP, many
// FRAMES apart, so the drain's same-pass pairing never saw a drag at all - a player looting a ghost's body
// produced an unpaired remove and a lone add published as our own move. That is the duplication decision 38
// exists to remove, and it survived E22b untouched.
//
// The repair is to pair by THE GAME'S OWN CURSOR STATE, not by elapsed time. Both RVAs are from
// RE_Kenshi/RVAs/Steam_1.0.65.br via tools/resolve_stub.py (.modding/01-environment.md "RVA source"):
//   slot 4124 ?pickupItemToMouse@InventoryGUI@@QEAA_NV?$basic_string@...@Z          -> 0x712DD0
//   slot 4125 ?placeItemFromMouse@InventoryGUI@@QEAA_NV?$basic_string@...TPoint...@Z -> 0x714990
// Both take the section name BY VALUE, which under the x64 ABI is a POINTER to a caller-built temporary the
// callee destroys (decomp_712dd0:38-44 and decomp_714990's tail both delete and reset it) - so the detours
// pass it straight through and never touch it. `_N` is bool, returned in al: `char`.
//
// WHERE "IS IT ON THE CURSOR" IS READ. NOT from InventoryGUI::getMouseItem (0x711680) - that function is
// misleadingly named and answers "which item is UNDER the mouse pointer in this window", by computing the
// hovered slot and calling getItemAt (decomp_711680). The real cursor state is a singleton the decompiles
// call MouseInventory (its constructor 0x70DA70 stores `MouseInventory::vftable`):
//   * the singleton POINTER lives at RVA 0x2131B18, lazily created by the accessor 0x6EE800 - so 0 is a
//     legitimate reading and means "nothing has ever been picked up", not a failure;
//   * +0x30 is the Item* ON THE CURSOR. 0x712990 writes it at pickup; 0x70CE50 clears it to 0; 0x714990
//     reads it as the item throughout (it reads +0x12C quantity and +0x124 functionKind off it).
//   * +0x40 is the source section name and +0x68 the source InventoryGUI - not read here: the source slot
//     comes off the ring's own REMOVE entry, which detour B already filled. Keeping a second copy in step by
//     hand is the trap lesson 11 is about.
// This field is re-read LIVE at every drain. Nothing in P6b starts a timer or measures an interval.
//
// WHAT A DRAG ACTUALLY PICKS UP, and it is not what one would assume (read out of decomp_712dd0:96-107).
// The pickup has TWO arms and the test is on the STACK SIZE:
//     if (item->quantity < 2 || DAT_1421323f9 || DAT_1421323f8)   -> InventorySection::removeItem(section, item)
//     else                                                        -> takeCertainAmountFrom(gui, item, 1)
// So a SINGLE item (quantity 1) - and a stack dragged with one of those two globals set, which by their use
// here are modifier keys, though THAT IS AN INFERENCE and not read - leaves through hook B and rings the
// op-1 REMOVE this mechanism pairs. **A plain drag on a STACK of two or more takes ONE UNIT through
// takeCertainAmountFrom instead.**
//
// CORRECTED 2026-09-04 (F540, P6i). THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY "a clone appears, removeItem is never called,
// HOOK D RINGS OP 3, and there is no remove to hold". THE MIDDLE CLAUSE IS WRONG and it was wrong in the
// direction that matters. `InventoryGUI::takeCertainAmountFrom` 0x70E670 is ten lines whole
