/* P7i (T233 section 3f) - A LONE GAME NEVER TRIES TO SEND AN ITEM OR A BOX MOVE, AND THESE TWO SAY SO.
   T233 measured `itemsDrain[sendFailed]` = 161 on a game with no transport: 161 box moves were verdicted
   kBoxMine through P6y's no-session fail-open arm and handed to net::SendItemMove, which has nothing to send
   through. P6z's RoleIsSingle() short-circuit had been added to the restock gate and not to the box road.
   Both roads are now cut at ItAddressee - the one place the detours decide whose inventory rang - so the ring
   never carries the entry, the area verdict is never asked, and every box counter and every drain counter
   reads 0 BY CONSTRUCTION rather than because a gate answered no.
   THESE TWO EXIST FOR LESSON 8. A row of zeros cannot tell "the hooks are dead" from "the hooks fired and a
   lone game correctly did nothing", and `restockSinglePlayer` is P6z's answer to exactly that question on the
   restock road. `itemSinglePlayer` counts moves in or out of a CHARACTER's inventory, `boxSinglePlayer` moves
   in or out of a BUILDING's, because those are different events and one number could not tell them apart.
   ANY THREAD (the four detours run on the engine's workers too), hence the interlocked increments. */
long long g_itemSinglePlayer = 0, g_boxSinglePlayer = 0;
// The apply's branches, one counter per op and per refusal reason.
long long g_appliedAdd = 0, g_appliedRemove = 0, g_appliedQty = 0, g_appliedSplit = 0;
long long g_applyNoInventory = 0, g_applyNoSection = 0, g_applyNoItemAtSlot = 0, g_applyNoContainer = 0;
// ---- P7a fold 1 (review-p6s H-2): THE MERGE'S ABSOLUTE QUANTITY, AND THE CELL IT USED TO CARRY ----
// `mergePublishedAtSlot`: an op 2 whose recorded cell was the CURSOR'S (a cursor clone is never filed, so it
// has no cell and reads 0,0) that was re-addressed to the SURVIVING stack's REAL slot and published there.
// `mergeUnresolved`: the same entry when the survivor could not be found in a live section at drain time -
// DROPPED. It is never sent at (0,0): the peer would write that count onto whatever happens to sit in the
// top-left cell of the named section, which is the corruption this fold exists to remove.
// `applyQtySidMismatch` is the RECEIVER'S half - see ApplyItemMove's op-2 arm.
long long g_mergePublishedAtSlot = 0, g_mergeUnresolved = 0, g_applyQtySidMismatch = 0;
// ---- P7a fold 2 (review-p6s H-1), CORRECTED BY P7g (review-p7a HIGH-1) ----
// `undoPublishedAdd`: merged-arm undos that put OUR OWN surviving stack back on the other player's screen
// with an op-0 ADD. The undo's own writes are raw POD stores that ring no hook (that is deliberate - it is
// what stops an echo loop), so without this send the peer keeps the REMOVE of our stack and never hears that
// it came back.
// THE NAME CARRIES TWO CAVEATS, BOTH PAID FOR BY review-p7a HIGH-1.
//   (1) `...Add` because P7a sent an op 2 here and an op 2 CANNOT WORK ON THIS PATH: it is an absolute
//       quantity written onto an object that must already be at the named cell, and the peer removed that
//       object earlier in the same send loop. Only an ADD re-creates it.
//   (2) IT COUNTS SENDS, NOT APPLIES. It is incremented when net::SendItemMove returns true - i.e. when the
//       bytes left this machine. Nothing acknowledges an ITEM_MOVE, so a receiver-side `undoApplied` cannot
//       be built without a new message; THE EVIDENCE THAT THE RESIDUE ACTUALLY CLOSES IS ON THE OTHER
//       MACHINE - the peer's `itemsApply[add]` rising in step with this number, and its
//       `itemsRefused[slotOccupied]` (with `slotOutOfRange` and `createFailed` beside it) NOT rising with it.
//       P7q (review-p7g M-1) CORRECTS THAT CONTROL. It used to name `itemsRefused[noItemAtSlot]`, which is a
//       counter THE op-0 PATH CANNOT REACH: applyNoItemAtSlot is booked at three sites and all three are the
//       op-1, op-2 and op-3 arms. An ADD is refused as slotOutOfRange, slotReadFault, slotOccupied,
//       createFailed, noContainer or fault - so the old sentence asked the run to read a constant. The name
//       was inherited from review-p7a's repair note, which was written about the op 2 this fold REPLACED,
//       where noItemAtSlot was the right counter. That evidence is owed by a T-run; it has not been taken,
//       and nothing in this file can take it.
// `undoAddNoRecord`: the same undo when the ADD half carried no 0x2A record (an empty base sid), so no ADD
// could be built. The receiver refuses an op-0 with no base sid outright, so sending one would be a lie in
// the log; the residue stands and this number says how often.
// P7q (review-p7g M-3): AND IT IS EXPECTED TO STAY 0, WHICH IS GOOD NEWS AND WAS NOT WRITTEN DOWN. The ADD
// detour drops any op-0 sighting whose record would not read (`if (fieldsWhy != 1) { ++g_itemReadFault;
// return; }`), so every op-0 entry that reaches the ring carries a record and `fields.baseSid` can only be
// empty for an item whose base-data sid is genuinely the empty string, which the engine does not produce.
// The arm is a guard, not a live failure mode: a NON-ZERO here means an item with an empty base sid exists,
// not that the undo has a hole. It is kept rather than deleted because without it an op-0 with no sid would
// be sent and the receiver would refuse it silently - a line in our log describing something that never
// reached the other screen.
long long g_undoPublishedAdd = 0, g_undoAddNoRecord = 0;
// ---- P7a fold 3 (review-p6y HIGH-1) ----
// `purseRefunded`: priced requests the holder REFUSED, or that lapsed, whose cats this game put back into
// (a buy) or took back out of (a sale) the player's own purse. The engine moved them locally before the hook
// that started all this even rang - read-prices 2a - so a refusal that touches only the item leaves the
// player poorer with nothing to show. `purseRefundFailed` is the same event when the purse could not be
// written or the character could not be found: the money stays wrong and the number says so.
long long g_purseRefunded = 0, g_purseRefundFailed = 0;
// ---- P7a fold 6 (review-p6t MEDIUM-3) ----
// `walkTruncated`: resolver walks in which `LoadedBuildings` hit one of its three silent limits - more than
// 64 active zones, a zone whose object array would not read (or held more than 20,000 things), or the 4096
// output cap. Under any of them a LIVE building is absent from the list, so the liveness test refuses a
// cache row that was right and books `boxCacheNotListed` - whose own comment says every one of those is a
// move into a building the engine no longer had. That sentence is only true when this number is zero.
// P7g (review-p7a M-2): THE OUTPUT-CAP TERM MEASURED THE WRONG QUANTITY AND IS FIXED IN THE CALLEE. It
// compared the zone's RAW THING COUNT against the room left in the output array, and only a small fraction
// of a zone's things are buildings - so it fired whenever a zone held more OBJECTS than there was room for
// BUILDINGS, whether or not one building was actually lost. This number would then read non-zero on a walk
// that dropped nothing, and `boxCacheNotListed`'s sentence would read as unreliable when it was fine, which
// is the mirror image of the defect review-p6t M-3 opened. It now counts BUILDINGS LOST: the flag is raised
// only when the inner loop stopped with things in that zone still unexamined.
long long g_walkTruncated = 0;
long long g_applyCreateFailed = 0, g_applyFault = 0, g_applyBadOp = 0;
// Why a slot was refused, kept apart from `fault`. slotOutOfRange: the wire named a cell this section does
// not have (ops 1/2/3), or an ADD whose footprint would run off the grid - the exact condition getItemAt
// and _addItem throw on. slotOccupied: the cell exists and the engine's own fitsAt said no.
// slotReadFault (E31 / review-p5x MEDIUM-4): the slot test could not be COMPUTED at all - the section's own
// width or height would not read back, its cell array is implausible, the item's own footprint would not
// read back, or the geometry came back non-positive. That is a memory-read failure on our side, not a bad
// coordinate from the wire, and booking it as slotOutOfRange reintroduced - in the other direction - exactly
// the conflation review-p5s HIGH-1 was built to remove.
long long g_slotOutOfRange = 0, g_slotOccupied = 0, g_slotReadFault = 0;
// What was dropped rather than carried, counted instead of silent. sidTooLong / secNameTooLong: the item's
// BASE record id (48-byte cap) or a section name (32-byte cap) longer than its buffer - THE WHOLE MOVE IS
// DROPPED, and that used to be indistinguishable from a memory fault.
// cosmeticSidDropped (E31 / review-p5x MEDIUM-2): a manufacturer, material or colour record id that did not
// fit its buffer. THE MOVE IS STILL CARRIED - the ghost gets the item without that one sub-record - and up
// to THREE can be booked for a single item, because ItSidOf is called three times per add. It used to share
// `sidTooLong` with the base sid, so `itemsDropped[sidTooLong]=6` could equally mean six moves lost or two
// moves carried intact with nothing lost, and nothing in the report told the two apart (lesson 1: verify
// what a number MEASURES). uniqueDropped: the created item would not take the isUnique flag the wire
// carried. splitBranchMismatch: the engine did not take the split branch the detour predicted, so nothing
// was rung.
long long g_sidTooLong = 0, g_cosmeticSidDropped = 0, g_secNameTooLong = 0, g_uniqueDropped = 0, g_splitBranchMismatch = 0;
long long g_teardowns = 0, g_teardownRingDropped = 0;
// ---- E22b counters (decision 38 part 2) ----
// `reverted` counts the local undos that succeeded; `revertFailed` the ones that could not be made safe, where
// NOTHING is sent and the local state is left exactly as E22a left it. `unpaired` counts removes out of an
// inventory we do not own that no add could be matched to - see the E22b note above ItCrossTransfer.
long long g_reqSent = 0, g_reqRecv = 0, g_reqConfirmed = 0, g_reqRefused = 0, g_reqTimedOut = 0;
long long g_reverted = 0, g_revertFailed = 0, g_unpaired = 0;
// The OWNER's refusal reasons, one counter each (a bare `refused` cannot tell "the slot was empty" from
// "we do not own that character", and those two want completely different repairs).
long long g_reqRefNotOwned = 0, g_reqRefNotLoaded = 0, g_reqRefNoSection = 0, g_reqRefNoItemAtSlot = 0;
long long g_reqRefShort = 0, g_reqRefUnreadable = 0, g_reqRefCreateFailed = 0, g_reqRefPlaceFailed = 0;
long long g_reqRefOutOfRange = 0, g_reqRefFault = 0, g_reqRefBadDir = 0, g_reqRefWrongItem = 0;   /* cell1: wrongItem */
// The REQUESTER's own book-keeping.
/* P7g (review-p7a M-6). ONE COUNTER, TWO EVENTS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM COSTS MONEY. `reqUnknownId` was
   incremented whenever a confirmation named a request row this game no longer holds, which happens for two
   completely different reasons:
     `reqUnknownIdLate`   - OUR OWN request, which OUR OWN 10 s timeout expired and refunded. The item is
                            never placed (that half is right and unchanged), but the refund has already been
                            paid, and if the confirmation says the holder COMPLETED the trade then the holder
                            really did move the item and really did settle the shopkeeper's cats - so the
                            refund was wrong and cats have been created (a purchase) or destroyed (a sale).
                            That case is `refundAfterCompletion`, counted and logged once per session.
     `reqUnknownIdForeign`- an answer to a request this game never made (or one whose row was reused). No
                            money was ever moved here for it and there is nothing to undo.
   The two are told apart by a small ring of ids this game's OWN timeout expired, written at the lapse and
   read here - see ItLapsedNote. A ring, not a flag, because several requests can lapse before any late
   answer arrives; 32 rows at 10 s each is far more history than a confirmation can plausibly be late by. */
long long g_reqSendFailed = 0, g_reqPendingFull = 0, g_reqRefusedHere = 0;
