// ---- E22b-3 counters (P6i): THE STACK DRAG ----
// `heldSplit` counts splits that were KEPT - the fifth hook named the stack and the clone, the engine's own
// cursor field still named that clone at the drain, and the stack belongs to a character or box this game
// does NOT write. `heldSplitPaired` the ones a later ADD carrying THE CLONE'S pointer claimed on an
// inventory we DO write (the cross-ownership TAKE - the shape that used to duplicate).
// `heldSplitCancelled` the ones the player put straight back onto the same stack. CORRECTED (review-p6i
// HIGH-2, P6s): this used to say the merge "arrives as an op-2 quantity change on THE ORIGINAL'S pointer",
// and NO SUCH ENTRY IS EVER RUNG - the engine merges INTO THE CURSOR ITEM, so the op 2 carries THE CLONE'S
// pointer, the original is removed and destroyed, and the enlarged clone is filed in the original's own
// cell. For as long as the search looked for the original's pointer this counter COULD NOT BE INCREMENTED AT
// ALL and every cancel closed as `heldSplitNotOurs`; a zero in it meant "not wired up", not "nobody
// cancelled a drag". It is recognised on the clone's pointer now (see the drain). Either way both ends are
// the ghost's, so nothing is reverted, requested or sent - the outcome was always right and only the record
// was wrong. ONE RESIDUE IS REAL and was never stated: after a cancel our mirror of that cell holds a
// DIFFERENT OBJECT than before the drag, because the original was destroyed and the clone took its place.
// Position-as-identity survives that; a pointer would not.
// `heldSplitNotOurs` the ones whose ADD landed on a second inventory we do not write either - the ghost
// moved its own unit and E22a drops that traffic anyway. NOTE, since HIGH-2 makes it visible: when that
// second inventory is a DIFFERENT STACK OF THE GHOST'S OWN, the merge destroys one of the ghost's objects on
// our screen too. Pre-existing E22a class (ghost-local traffic is never reconciled), recorded here so the
// line is not read as "nothing happened".
// `heldSplitLost` the ones the cursor stopped naming with no ADD and no merge.
// THE FIVE ARE DISJOINT and heldSplit == paired + cancelled + notOurs + lost + (at most one still waiting).
long long g_heldSplit = 0, g_heldSplitPaired = 0, g_heldSplitLost = 0, g_heldSplitCancelled = 0;
long long g_heldSplitNotOurs = 0;
// What was seen but NOT kept, so a zero in `heldSplit` can be read rather than guessed at.
// `splitMineUnpublished`: units dragged off OUR OWN stack (F540: no hook rings on the stack) whose new count
// was NOT published - ItOwnSplitFlush publishes it at the end of the drain that saw the split, from a live
// read, and counts here only a stack that was no longer the object in its own square, or whose count would
// not read (its own later changes speak for it then). `splitNoOwner`: the stack's owner could not be addressed (no
// replicated uid, or a box with no instance id). `splitMissed`: a published split whose clone was no longer
// the cursor item by the time the drain FIRST looked - the whole drag happened inside one tick. The row is
// still taken (its ADD may be in that very ring) and released at the end of the drain if nothing claimed it,
// so this number OVERLAPS the five above: it is a diagnostic, not one of the disjoint five.
long long g_splitMineUnpublished = 0, g_splitNoOwner = 0, g_splitMissed = 0;
/* AN ITEM THIS GAME'S OWN PLAYER LIFTS OUT OF A CONTAINER THIS GAME WRITES (src/common/ownpick.h) - ownPick[...]: atPickup (a whole
   item's removal sent when it was lifted), splitAtPickup (a stack's new count sent after units were lifted off it), dropSkipped /
   releaseSkipped (the drop, or the cursor letting go of it with no drop, found that removal already sent), backAdd (a drop into a
   container another game writes left the item back in ours - its add sent), backGone (nothing of it was left there to publish - it
   left with the request), backUnread (it was there but would not read - nothing sent), sendFailed (any of these sends failed). */
const int kOpAtPickup = 0, kOpSplitAtPickup = 1, kOpDropSkipped = 2, kOpReleaseSkipped = 3, kOpBackAdd = 4, kOpBackGone = 5,
          kOpBackUnread = 6, kOpSendFailed = 7, kOpCount = 8;
long long g_ownPick[kOpCount] = { 0 };
/* Units lifted off a stack this game writes, at the drop (ownpick::SplitDropRoad) - ownSplitDrop[give,own,kept,released]: give (into a
   container another game writes - sent down the give road), own (into one of our own containers - published as our own move), kept
   (into another game's container that nobody can be asked about - the drop stands), released (the clone left the cursor with no ADD
   this game saw: the ground, or merged back onto our own stack). */
const int kOsdGive = 0, kOsdOwn = 1, kOsdKept = 2, kOsdReleased = 3, kOsdCount = 4;
long long g_ownSplitDrop[kOsdCount] = { 0 };
int g_ownPickLines = 0;
const int kOwnPickLineCap = 300;
// The one shape the split undo can fail in HALFWAY: the clone was taken out of our side and destroyed,
// and the stack's count could not then be written back. Nothing is sent (the request goes out only after
// an undo that returned 1), so the owner's real stack is untouched and only our mirror is short - but a
// unit has left this game's screen and this is the only number that says so.
long long g_splitUnitLost = 0;
// The fifth hook's own book-keeping. `takeClamp` is the SECOND CALLER named above (the trade clamp in
// placeItemFromMouse) and is NOT a failure. It REPLACES `takeNotInInventory`, which review-p6i HIGH-3
// showed could not fire: it tested `Item+0xD8`, which the pickup sets on the cursor item on both arms. The
// report token is `clampEmptySec`, after the thing that actually decides it, so the number cannot be read
// without its mechanism. `splitDisplaced`: the THIRD state - a swap-displaced cursor item, `+0xD8 == 0` -
// counted where it happens and deciding nothing; it OVERLAPS `takeClamp` (a displaced item reaching the
// clamp is booked under both) and is a diagnostic, not one of the exits. `takeNotSplit`: the call did not
// describe a partial take at all (howmany outside 0 < n < quantity, or no clone came back).
// `takeNoInventory`: the GUI would not say which inventory it is showing. `takeQtyMismatch`: the original's
// count did not drop by `howmany` after the call, so the engine did not do what this hook is built on -
// kept apart from `splitBranchMismatch`, which is hook D's version of the same question about a different
// function (lesson 1).
long long g_seenTakeCertain = 0, g_takeClamp = 0, g_takeNotSplit = 0;
long long g_splitDisplaced = 0;
long long g_takeNoInventory = 0, g_takeQtyMismatch = 0;
long long g_thrTakeMain = 0, g_thrTakeOff = 0;
// ---- review-p6i folds (P6s) ----
// HIGH-1, the split undo's three arms. `splitUndoMerged`: the clone had absorbed a stack of OURS, so the
// ghost's stack was raised by the units taken and the merged object was REDUCED by them - the player's own
// units stayed where they put them and nothing was destroyed. This is the arm that used to write the ghost
// up by our whole stack and then destroy our units, and it is an ORDINARY action, not an edge case.
// `splitUndoShort`: the clone held FEWER units than were taken (the trade clamp took part of it away
// again), so only what was there could be moved back and our mirror of the ghost is short by the
// difference. Both are counted as what happened, not as failures: the undo succeeded in both.
long long g_splitUndoMerged = 0, g_splitUndoShort = 0;
// HIGH-2, the cancel's own inputs, so a zero in `cancelled` can be interrogated instead of guessed at
// (lesson 12). `splitCancelMerge`: an op 2 naming the CLONE arrived on the original's owner and section -
// the shape a cancel makes. `splitCancelNoRemove`: one of those where the ORIGINAL'S own op-1 REMOVE did
// NOT ring, so it was a merge into a DIFFERENT stack of the ghost's and not a cancel at all.
// `splitCancelSlotAgree`: of the merges seen, how many also carried the original's cell. It is expected to
// be ZERO - the clone is constructed and never filed, so it has no cell - and it is measured rather than
// assumed, because requiring the cell is exactly what would rebuild HIGH-2.
long long g_splitCancelMerge = 0, g_splitCancelNoRemove = 0, g_splitCancelSlotAgree = 0;
// MEDIUM-1: one drop can do the trade clamp AND a swap, in that order (decomp_714990:460 then :602-608), and
// `splitRan` alone then read as "a leftover is on the cursor" when a real displaced item was. This counts
// the combination, so the shape has a number the first time it happens.
long long g_clampAndSwap = 0;
// MEDIUM-4: the pairing table could not seat a key. Unreachable while the table is twice the ring - counted
// rather than assumed, because an unreachable branch that is never counted cannot be shown to be unreached.
long long g_pairTableFull = 0;
// ---- review-p6b folds (P6i) ----
// HIGH-1: `heldSwapped` counts drops onto an OCCUPIED cell where the engine put the DISPLACED item on the
// cursor (decomp_714990:604, :634) and the held slot was re-armed for it, so a swap out of a ghost's grid is
// a held take-out like any other instead of an untracked item. `placeLeftover` is the trade clamp's
// remainder arriving on the cursor by the same route - it was never in an inventory, so no REMOVE will ever
// ring for it and re-arming would be inert; it is counted and the slot is cleared. `heldStageLost` is a
// second held row that had nowhere to go because the first was still standing at the end of the drain.
long long g_heldSwapped = 0, g_placeLeftover = 0, g_heldStageLost = 0;
// MEDIUM-3: `heldLost` KEEPS ITS MEANING - every held row released because the cursor stopped naming its
// object - and is now split by WHOSE item it was, because the two halves are not the same event.
// `heldLostHarmless`: the item was OURS, so the remove we had suppressed is published here and E22a's
// behaviour is restored in full; nothing diverges. `heldLostDup`: the item was a GHOST'S. Nothing was
// published, nothing reverted and nothing requested, so our mirror of that inventory is short by what the
// player took while the owner's real inventory is untouched - and if the unit landed anywhere our side
// announces, it exists twice. STATED PRECISELY: this counts THE SHAPE THAT CAN DUPLICATE, not a duplication
// observed. `unpaired` is untouched by this and keeps the meaning E22b gave it.
long long g_heldLostHarmless = 0, g_heldLostDup = 0;
// MEDIUM-4: both rollbacks re-read who owns the character AT THE MOMENT THEY WRITE, and this counts the
// times that answer had changed since the request was granted (forced handoff on sector unload is a standing
// feature of this project, and the pending row lives 10 s).
// review-p6i MEDIUM-3 (P6s): ONE counter served TWO events - the TAKE rollback, which leaves an object in no
// inventory, and the GIVE revoke, which leaves nothing behind - so it could not answer the question the
// parity register asks with it (how many objects have been leaked). They are separate numbers now, and the
// TAKE arm books `rollbackTakeFailed` as the GIVE arm already booked `rollbackGiveFailed`, so
// `rollbackTake + rollbackTakeFailed` accounts for every TAKE row that ends.
long long g_rollbackTakeNotOursNow = 0, g_rollbackGiveNotOursNow = 0;
// review-p6i MEDIUM-2 (P6s): OF THOSE, the ones where the row was a STORAGE BOX. The P6i re-read was guarded
// `isBox == 0` and so covered characters only, while a box's holder is the game holding its patch of map and
// moves for exactly the same reason - `boxHeldRestale` exists because this file already knows it. The box's
// holder is re-read here from the engine (its building resolved by the key the request was granted on, its
// position read, and the SAME `ItBoxVerdict` the drain asks - AreaViewTS for that sector), not from anything
// remembered when the request was granted.
// P7a (review-p6s M-1). ONE COUNTER, TWO EVENTS - and it was added in the same commit that split
// `rollbackNotOursNow` for exactly this reason. The TAKE rollback LEAVES AN OBJECT IN NO INVENTORY; the GIVE
// revoke leaves nothing behind at all. A reader asking "how many box rollbacks stranded an item" could not
// answer it from the shared number, and the parity register named it under the TAKE entry alone, which
// attributed every count to the half that leaks. Two numbers, one per event.
long long g_takeRollbackBoxNotHolder = 0, g_giveRevokeBoxNotHolder = 0;
// P7a (review-p6s M-2). THE VERDICT THAT IS NOT AN OWNERSHIP ANSWER. `ItBoxHolderNow` collapsed every
// non-mine verdict into "not ours", so a box whose patch of map is merely NOT LOADED HERE - the ordinary
// consequence of walking away - was reported as an authority handoff and its object was kept, in no
// inventory, for a game that was never asked to put it back. Only a REAL other-holder answer (kBoxHeld, or a
// fresh map naming nobody) keeps the object now; a zone-gone / unloading / no-answer / unresolvable box
// falls through to the ordinary failure arm, which destroys what it cannot re-add and says so.
/* P7g (review-p7a M-1). SPLIT PER EVENT, for the reason stated four lines above about the counter beside
   it - and this one was added in the very commit that made that argument. The TAKE rollback and the GIVE
   revoke reach it from different sides with different consequences, and the parity register named it under
   the TAKE entry alone, so every count in it was being attributed to the half that can strand an object.
   WHAT NEITHER NUMBER SEPARATES, STATED SO IT IS NOT MISREAD AS CLOSED (review-p7a M-5): the split is by
   SIDE, not by VERDICT. Both still cover kBoxZoneGone, kBoxUnload, kBoxNoAnswer and kBoxNoKey together, and
   kBoxNoAnswer is a different animal from the other three - it means no fresh map can speak for the sector
   (a relay flap), not that the box is absent here, so it usually falls through to a re-add that SUCCEEDS
   locally and publishes nothing. review-p7a M-5's note that M-1's split "would do anyway" is refuted: it
   would not, and M-5 is still open. */
long long g_takeRollbackBoxZoneGone = 0, g_giveRevokeBoxZoneGone = 0;
// ---- E22c counters (decision 38 part 3, decision 40) ----
// `ownerNotChar` KEEPS ITS MEANING - every sighting whose Inventory owner is established and is not a
// Character - but it is no longer the same thing as "dropped": a BOX is now rung.
// review-p6e MEDIUM-5, AND THE LABEL SAYS SO NOW. Before P6e this counter meant "a move we DROPPED because
// the owner is a container"; since P6e it also counts every box move that was rung, published and applied
// SUCCESSFULLY. F534's recorded numbers (host 340, client 857) were taken under the old meaning, so THE
// SERIES DOES NOT COMPARE ACROSS THE P6e BOUNDARY. The report token reads `ownerNotCharInclBoxesAndFacades`
// (P6y: it said `ownerNotCharInclBoxesRung` here, which P6o had already renamed in the string itself) for
// that reason - a name that carries its caveat cannot be read without it (lesson 1) - and
