// place the counter for it would have gone. T230 measured keyEmpty A=32 B=1: a box the engine has never
// written to a zone file carries an EMPTY id, so it cannot be named on the wire in either direction. The
// engine mints one inside SavedObjectState's constructor 0x386BE0, which Building::serialise 0x551890 runs
// (containers.md). Calling that path for ONE building was examined and REFUSED, on four grounds, each read
// rather than assumed:
//   (a) IT WOULD NOT MAKE THE TWO GAMES AGREE, which is the whole point of a key. The minted string is
//       `<n>-<the writing container's own name>-INGAME` (SavedObjectState::newInstanceId 0x386630 reads
//       the container's name at +0x150). A scratch container is not the box's zone file, so the id would name
//       the wrong file - and each game would mint its own from its own counter, so the two would differ. The
//       ids that DO agree across two games agree because they come from a shared file (F544(a)).
//   (b) IT WRITES INTO THE PLAYER'S WORLD. The constructor calls GameData::addInstance 0x6C2AF0 to
//       put an ObjectInstance into the container's "0-buildinglist", writes the id back onto the Building and
//       sets InstanceID::modified = 1. The only scratch container this file can reach is BORROWED from a
//       platoon (ItScratchContainer / ItAnyScratchContainer, and clothing.cpp:163-169's caveat), so the
//       phantom instance would sit in a container the engine serialises on its own schedule.
